trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Key lab findings:  
- Markedly elevated IgE (1250 IU/mL — far above normal for age, which is typically <30–60 IU/mL in toddlers);  
- Low IgA (48 mg/dL; normal for 3-year-old: ~20–100 mg/dL — borderline low but notable in context);  
- Normal IgG/IgM;  
- Lymphocyte subsets show high absolute T-cell count (2800/μL — upper limit of normal for age is ~3000, so within high-normal range), preserved B and NK cells, and normal CD4:CD8 ratio.  
This profile — severe eczema + recurrent infections + failure to thrive + lymphadenopathy + hepatosplenomegaly + extreme hyper-IgE — is classic for *autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome (AD-HIES, STAT3 deficiency)* or *DOCK8 deficiency*. But DOCK8 typically shows low IgM and declining T/B cells with age — here IgM is normal and lymphocyte counts are robust. STAT3 deficiency better fits: high IgE, eczema, staphylococcal infections, retained lymphocytes, and non-immunologic features (e.g., retained primary teeth, scoliosis) — though those haven’t been assessed yet. To distinguish, I need imaging for skeletal features (e.g., scoliosis on spine X-ray) and dental exam — but those are less urgent than confirming immune function. Next highest-yield test is *specific antibody response* (e.g., tetanus/diphtheria titers post-vaccination) to assess functional humoral immunity — often impaired in STAT3 deficiency despite normal IgG.

[TEST] Please obtain:  
- Serum anti-tetanus toxoid IgG and anti-diphtheria toxoid IgG levels (to assess functional antibody response to protein antigens).

